return [====[nd reveals Veil. Failed activation is free; once committed, interruption or a miss does not refund Magic or reset the cooldown. Removal, death, role or dungeon changes, forced movement, Held or Muted cancel it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 37</div></article><article class="page" id="heavy-plumber" data-title="Boots of the Heavy Plumber"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Heavy Plumber</h2><table><tr><td>Heavy Stomp</td><td>Land from above · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>These Rare-or-better boots occupy your feet slot. Equip them before leaving solid ground, then land on an enemy from above to deal 2d6 plus Strength as physical damage. Your usual dice, defenses and damage modifiers apply. Sideways contact and landing on allies do not attack.</p><p>A valid stomp gives you a short upward rebound, even if the enemy dodges or blocks the damage. Low ceilings reduce the rebound. With insufficient clearance or unsafe floor support below, you land normally without an attack. The Boots do not grant flight, invulnerability or protection from fall damage.</p><p>You get one stomp attempt per airborne excursion, with at least 0.65 seconds between commitments. Your bounce is part of that same excursion. Land on solid, non-actor support to rearm; standing on an enemy or swapping Boots in midair will not do it.</p><p>Heavy Stomp needs no arrow sequence or Magic. Muted and holding a potion do not disable it; Held does. A committed stomp reveals Veil. Removing the Boots or changing life, role or dungeon invalidates the armed landing.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 38</div></article><article class="page" id="magic-hourglass" data-title="A little more time"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little more time</h2><table><tr><td>Magic Hourglass</td><td>LMB / RMB: USE · Adds 2d4 minutes · Stack of 3</td></tr></table><p>This rare consumable buys the whole party more time. Equip and hold it in the Throwable slot, then press either mouse button. One Hourglass is consumed; the server rolls two ordinary four-sided dice and adds their sum in minutes to the current dungeon clock. The Die Log shows both dice, and everyone&#x27;s clock updates immediately.</p><p>The result is always two through eight minutes: no exploding dice, class bonuses or Magic cost. Any Hero class can use it, with the ordinary 0.6-second item cooldown. A stack holds at most three, and each legitimate use adds its full result even above 30:00.</p><p>Use it before time runs out. At or after the deadline, TIME OVER wins; the Hourglass cannot reverse the collapse. Paused staging clocks, failed or cleared dungeons, Soldier bodies and unequipped items cannot spend it. Successful rescue resets the next dungeon to 30:00, while unused Hourglasses remain in your Hero&#x27;s inventory.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 39</div></article><article class="page" id="moon-boots" data-title="Boots of the Moon"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Moon</h2><table><tr><td>Moon Gravity</td><td>25% lower gravity · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip these Rare-or-better boots in your feet slot for higher jumps and longer airtime in the dungeon. Gravity is reduced by 25%; ordinary steering and horizontal movement remain yours to control. No arrow sequence, Magic cost or cooldown is required.</p><p>Your usual jump, Cloud Step and Wall Jump impulses still apply. Float On keeps its own controls and Magic cost. Invisible maze walls, locked gates, collisions and fall damage remain in force: these boots do not grant flight or invulnerability.</p><p>Removing or replacing the boots restores ordinary gravity, including in midair. Their effect ends on death, staging or role change and returns when the same equipped boots belong to a living, deployed Hero again. Human Soldiers cannot borrow the Hero&#x27;s equipment.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 40</div></article><article class="page" id="tanuki-ring" data-title="Tanuki’s Ring"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Tanuki’s Ring</h2><table><tr><td>Statue</td><td>Stand still for 2 seconds · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip this Rare-or-better ring in either hand and stand still on solid ground for two continuous seconds. You become stone: enemies stop seeing and targeting you, and direct hits, explosions, hazards and damaging statuses cannot take your HP while Statue remains active.</p><p>Movement immediately ends Statue, including jumps, crouching, forced propulsion, moving supports and teleportation. Attacking, casting Magic, using an item or interacting also ends it, even if the action fails. Dealing HP damage ends it too. You must stand still for another full two seconds; looking around alone is allowed.</p><p>Statue costs no Magic and has no fixed duration or cooldown. It does not cure existing conditions or stop the dungeon collapse clock. Removing or moving the ring, dying, changing role or changing dungeon cancels it. An interrupted connection cannot retain an old activation.</p><p>Other players can see your stone body. A brief original transformation chime and stone flourish mark activation, and STATUE appears above your status portrait. Separate sources of invisibility retain their own rules.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 41</div></article><article class="page" id="wand" data-title="A little borrowed sorcery"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little borrowed sorcery</h2><table><tr><td>Wand</td><td>LMB: piercing Beam · 12 charges · No reload</td></tr><tr><td>Wizard</td><td>Automatic class permission</td></tr><tr><td>Rogue</td><td>d100 ≤ 5 × Combat Level, capped at95</td></tr><tr><td>Fighter</td><td>May carry; cannot activate</td></tr></table><p>Each Wand is a distinct procedural weapon with its own twelve-charge pool. Equip a copy through Equipment and press LMB to attempt a Beam. One accepted attempt spends one charge and no personal Magic, with at least0.65 seconds between attempts. RMB still casts your selected Spellbook Magic.</p><p>Wizards activate Wands normally. Rogues make one non-exploding d100 roll:5% success at Level1,50% at Level10,95% at Levels19 and20. A failed Rogue attempt still consumes the charge and cooldown but produces no Beam. Other classes cannot activate it. Muted and Intimidated prevent activation without spending a charge.</p><p>A successful Wand Beam deals3d6 plus Wisdom through the shared Magic combat rules. It pierces eligible bodies once each in order and stops at blocking architecture or its normal range, extended by your existing spatial bonuses. Its cyan core keeps the familiar Beam identity; the Wand affinity supplies elemental damage and color accents.</p><p>The Wand uses its rolled equipment status chances and normal saves after effective damage; it does not add a second automatic Content rider. Your normal compatible damage modifiers and defenses still apply. Wand use does not unlock Beam or a Content in the Spellbook, and spending a charge does not count as spending personal Magic.</p><p>The remaining charges are always shown while the Wand is held and in its Equipment description. Empty Wands remain in your inventory. Reloading, ammunition pickups, regeneration, swapping copies, weapon recreation, respawning and dungeon transitions never refill a copy. Stored and frozen copies preserve their recorded charges. Attack attempts reveal Veil and end Statue.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 42</div></article><article class="page" id="death" data-title="Down, but not finished"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Down, but not finished</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>Heroes start with three lives. On death, your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions stay with your Hero for the respawn. Your character identity, XP, class, feats, and persistent DFT collection remain too. Reconnecting does not restore a lost life or reroll your character.</p><p>A normal death has a mandatory 20-second respawn lockout. Play Tetris while waiting, or spectate. After the lockout, press F to finalize your reward and respawn. You can keep playing until the normal 60-second death-Tetris maximum, when respawn happens automatically.</p><p>At zero lives, press F3 for three choices: wait for resurrection, begin a new Level-1 Hero, or join the Soldiers. Beginning again discards that character&#x27;s carried gear and progression but preserves account $DEB and DFTs. All Heroes may be eliminated without resetting the dungeon; only the collapse timer ends a failed run. Someone must still reach the rescue objective to clear it.</p><p>Successful rescue can bring eliminated teammates back under the campaign&#x27;s comeback rules, up to the maximum of four lives. A revived Hero returns with the same saved weapons, ammunition, wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 43</div></article><article class="page" id="tetris" data-title="Make room for a comeback"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make room for a comeback</h2><table><tr><td>One line</td><td>+10 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Two lines at once</td><td>+30 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Three lines at once</td><td>+50 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Four lines at once</td><td>+80 temporary HP</td></tr></table><p>Move pieces left or right, rotate with Up/Forward, soft-drop with Down/Back, and hard-drop with Jump. Clearing several lines at once earns the corresponding reward, not the sum of separate single-line clears.</p><p>Rewards become temporary HP overfill. They do not permanently increase maximum HP and cannot be refilled by ordinary regeneration. Topping out ends the board session while preserving the bonus already earned; it does not erase the mandatory death wait.</p><p>After rescue, Victory Tetris lasts 20 seconds. Russian Asset doubles qualifying HP rewards in both Tetris modes. It also raises the death-Tetris maximum to 120 seconds, but F still becomes available after the same 20-second lockout. It never extends Victory Tetris or the active dungeon clock.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 44</div></article><article class="page" id="soldiers" data-title="Friends—and human foes"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Friends—and human foes</h2><p>Up to four players take cooperative Hero roles. Up to six additional players can control enemy Soldiers, for ten active players; further arrivals spectate. Role availability and the Soldier queue determine when an incarnation can be entered.</p><p>A human Soldier receives a generated monster character, class, abilities, and automatically selected feats. The player does not draft Hero feats. A fresh incarnation is a fresh Soldier character, not the revival of the previous build.</p><p>Human Soldiers can earn incarnation-local XP and automatic Combat Levels, capped by Dungeon Level +3. AI Soldiers do not earn XP. Monster-side Levels can reach 999. Soldier play preserves your eliminated Hero; return to the Hero Queue to become eligible for resurrection.</p><p>The Debbie Fund can reward Soldier-attributable Hero life losses after a successful rescue. Switching into Soldier play forfeits that player&#x27;s Hero share for that dungeon. The fund does not award a rescue pool for a timeout.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 45</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-basics" data-title="Meet the prison staff"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Meet the prison staff</h2><p>An enemy&#x27;s archetype tells you how it fights; its class, Combat Level, feats, and element make that particular creature different. Normal tier chances are 60% Typical, 30% Elite, and 10% Champion, using Dungeon Level, +1, and +2 respectively. A tier is a Level change, not a secret second set of damage multipliers.</p><p>Shamblers threaten close quarters; Runners close gaps quickly. Deadcrabs are low jumping attackers. Soldiers use ranged fire; Blitzers press aggressively; Snipers punish exposed sight lines.</p><p>BioBlasters threaten with biological attacks. Watchers demand attention to their readable attack tells. Seekers telegraph a committed charge: move out of its path instead of retreating along it.</p><p>Listen, look ahead, and use corners. Killing an enemy does not guarantee a permanently safe floor; wandering populations can replenish. Size, class tint, elemental aura, and aimed information help you decide what to tackle first.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 46</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-new" data-title="More unwelcome company"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>More unwelcome company</h2><table><tr><td>Climber</td><td>Pink fast-zombie silhouette; climbs to reach you. Watch vertical approaches.</td></tr><tr><td>Nodule</td><td>Stationary gas hazard. Do not stand beside it.</td></tr><tr><td>Flamer</td><td>Red soldier silhouette; warns before a short-range fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Big Crab</td><td>Oversized headcrab with a telegraphed fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Sentry</td><td>Stationary gun emplacement; break its firing line.</td></tr><tr><td>Razor</td><td>Flying blade with a warned dive. Move off its committed approach.</td></tr><tr><td>Arc Caster</td><td>Marks a target area before its Magic strike. Leave the mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Lurker</td><td>Ceiling ambusher with a venom shot. Look up.</td></tr><tr><td>Beam Sweeper</td><td>Stationary sweeping laser. Interrupt its charge or use cover; gunfire will not cancel an already released beam.</td></tr></table><p>Fire can attempt Immolated; venom can attempt Poisoned. These enemies use the same saves, damage, elements, and progression rules as the rest of the roster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 47</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-control" data-title="The control detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The control detail</h2><table><tr><td>Gaoler</td><td>Icy-blue Vortigaunt. Its Ice attack marks a fixed patch of floor before erupting. Leave the mark or interrupt the charge: a failed Wisdom save can leave you Held while its Runner closes in.</td></tr><tr><td>Silencer</td><td>Pale-ivory Combine elite. A visible line warns of a straight, non-homing Light bolt. Sidestep or take cover. A failed Wisdom save can leave you Muted; guns and melee remain available.</td></tr><tr><td>Repulsor</td><td>Brown-and-gold Vortigaunt. It warns before an Earth pulse around its fixed casting position. Leave the ring or take cover. The hit can push you into a Soldier’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists appear with ordinary attackers. Identify the control threat early, then choose whether to interrupt it, evade its commitment, or remove its companion.</p><p>Held and Muted use the ordinary saves and recovery rules. Their warning colors identify the attack; a creature’s class and elemental affinity still belong to that individual. Cover and movement remain useful answers for every class.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 48</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-support" data-title="The prison support detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The prison support detail</h2><table><tr><td>Stitcher</td><td>A slave Vortigaunt channels recovery into one wounded ally. The green link announces a 1.5-second window to interrupt it or break its line of sight. Each completed channel restores 12% of the ally’s maximum HP, rounded up and capped at 24 HP; it cannot revive the dead.</td></tr><tr><td>Bulwark</td><td>A Combine elite protects one ally with a blue tether. Its six-second guard adds 25 percentage points to ordinary physical Block, still capped at 33%. Magic bypasses Block. Kill or interrupt the Bulwark, draw the ally more than 240 units away, or break the tether with cover.</td></tr><tr><td>Cantor</td><td>A Metrocop calls up to three nearby allies onto one visible Hero for four seconds. The gold call changes their target priority without adding damage or speed. Break sightlines, interrupt the caller, or turn the pursuing group into your team’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These enemies support existing companions; they neither summon reinforcements nor revive fallen enemies. Support does not stack. When nobody qualifies for support, each specialist can still fire a weak ordinary projectile.</p><p>Their distinctive links and call warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class and elemental affinity are independent of the support role.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 49</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-pursuit" data-title="Watch the other route"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch the other route</h2><table><tr><td>Pincer</td><td>A purple Combine soldier takes a legal alternate approach around the lane where it saw you, using a short loop or a two-leg flank within its own room. Its split chevron gives a brief warning before it moves. Watch the side approach, change lanes, or interrupt it; it cannot cross a locked gate or follow an unseen change of direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Harrier</td><td>A cyan Metrocop commits a visible firing line and releases one straight projectile before withdrawing. Its backward chevrons announce the retreat. Dodge the shot, then close the gap or deny its cover; interrupting the wind-up prevents the planned withdrawal.</td></tr><tr><td>Waylayer</td><td>An amber Combine elite marks a reachable junction with a square and cross. You have one second before it starts walking there. Take a different exit, interrupt it, or beat it to the junction. The marker deals no damage; its ordinary gunfire still warns before release.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists move through ordinary passages. Their plans are brief commitments to the position where they last saw a Hero, not knowledge of movement behind walls. Gates, obstructions, disabling conditions and interrupted plans can foil their maneuver.</p><p>Pincers appear beside Soldiers, Harriers beside Shamblers, and Waylayers beside Runners. Their route warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class, health and elemental affinity still belong to the individual.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 50</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-reactions" data-title="Make their response your opening"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make their response your opening</h2><table><tr><td>Pavise</td><td>The steel-blue Combine elite braces behind a shield-shaped tell. After a short warning, its guard stays aimed in one direction for 2.5 seconds. Frontal physical attacks face an extra Block chance, capped at 33%; it cannot walk or shoot while braced. Use Magic, circle to a flank, interrupt it, or wait for the guard to end. The ground arc shows the protected direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Repriser</td><td>The rose-colored Metrocop answers a direct damaging hit with a hooked-arrow warning and one straight projectile. Its one-second wind-up gives you time to sidestep, take cover or interrupt it with another damaging hit. It does not reflect your damage, and damage over time does not trigger the response.</td></tr><tr><td>Redliner</td><td>The rust-red Combine soldier normally fires from range. A direct hit that leaves it at 40% health or less can provoke one wounded rush: a jagged chevron announces the approach, followed by a one-second warning along its fixed lunge direction. Step aside or obstruct it. The lunge has three seconds of stationary recovery, shown by open bars; healing to 60% health rearms a later rush.</td></tr></table><p>Pavises appear with Runners, Reprisers with Soldiers, and Redliners with Shamblers. Watch both members of the pair: their different attack rhythms create different openings.</p><p>These responses keep ordinary class defenses, statuses and damage rules. Held stops the guard and lunge; Muted does not stop physical shots. Their warnings remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 51</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-patterns" data-title="Read the whole shot path"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the whole shot path</h2><table><tr><td>Caromer</td><td>The cyan Combine soldier draws a shot path with a diamond at its possible wall bank. After a 1.1-second warning, the physical projectile can ricochet once along the reflected line. Dodge the first lane and watch the second. New cover absorbs it; without a suitable wall it fires straight.</td></tr><tr><td>Reeler</td><td>The amber Metrocop warns along a fixed lane, then sends a shot beyond the position where it saw you. The shot pauses for three quarters of a second, marked by a hollow diamond, before returning to its original launch point. Sidestep, then stay clear until the return passes; cover stops either leg.</td></tr><tr><td>Forker</td><td>The mint-green Combine elite draws two parallel lanes for 1.2 seconds. Its paired shots leave the center open: hold that gap or move completely outside the lanes. There is no hidden middle shot. Cover and an interrupted wind-up can stop the volley.</td></tr></table><p>Caromers appear with Shamblers, Reelers with Runners, and Forkers with Soldiers. Companions can pressure the safe space, so read both attack rhythms. These are physical shots governed by ordinary class defenses and conditions.</p><p>Hit-stun can cancel the warning. A shot already in flight keeps its frozen path; it never follows a moving Hero. Each pattern can damage a given Hero at most once, including both Forker lanes. Reduced effects retain the path warnings.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 52</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-traps" data-title="Leave yourself an escape"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Leave yourself an escape</h2><table><tr><td>Wirewright</td><td>The cyan-and-orange Combine soldier marks a short tripwire across a lane. A hollow diamond and thin boundaries give 1.25 seconds of warning before it becomes live for five seconds. Endpoint posts and the upper rail show its jumpable height: jump above the wire, walk around either end, wait it out, or interrupt its maker. The narrow floor outline shows the full danger strip.</td></tr><tr><td>Snarer</td><td>The blue Vortigaunt arms a fixed circular snare after one second. It stays armed for at most six seconds. Entering with enough time left triggers one snap 1.25 seconds later, announced by inward teeth and a shrinking bar. Leave the circle to bait the snap safely; it cannot follow you or reset its timer. The Ice hit can leave you Held under the ordinary save rules. Mute, interrupt or defeat the Snarer to cancel it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cordon</td><td>The orange floor turret is a destructible pressure node. Two concentric rings warn for 1.5 seconds before one physical pulse, followed by a brief recovery. Enter the safe center, leave the outer ring, take cover, or interrupt or destroy the node to open the approach. The center stays safe; this is a single pulse, not lingering gas.</td></tr></table><p>Wirewrights appear with Runners, Snarers with Soldiers, and Cordons with Shamblers. Their companions pressure different escape routes; watch the whole encounter before committing.</p><p>These traps stay where they were marked. Thin boundaries and a hollow diamond mean arming; thicker boundaries mean live. Their finite warnings and escape routes remain visible with reduced effects.</p><p>A trap can hit a given Hero only once. Damage, defenses and conditions follow the ordinary combat rules. Interrupting or destroying the maker cancels its trap; removal never leaves a hidden hazard behind.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 53</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-melee-spacing" data-title="Respect the second swing"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Respect the second swing</h2><table><tr><td>Reaper</td><td>The green-tinted zombie marks a broad semicircle in front of itself for 1.1 seconds, then sweeps it once. Back beyond the arc, circle behind it, take cover or interrupt. Its facing stays fixed through the warning; exploit the 2.5-second stationary recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Drubber</td><td>The ochre fast zombie marks two nested, narrow attack sectors. After one second it strikes the inner sector; 0.85 seconds later it strikes the longer outer sector along the same fixed direction. Sidestep and wait for both beats. Rushing straight back after the first swing can put you into the second. It then recovers for 2.8 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Fencer</td><td>The pale-violet Metrocop shows a dashed retreat path and steps backward, then warns for 0.9 seconds before a long, narrow thrust from its marked endpoint. Resist chasing straight after it; move sideways out of the rectangle. Obstructing the retreat or interrupting the warning stops the attack. It recovers for 2.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Reapers appear with Soldiers, Drubbers with Runners, and Fencers with Shamblers. Their companions can contest the space you need to escape, so read the entire encounter.</p><p>All three make physical melee attacks under ordinary damage and defense rules. Interrupting them ends the commitment. Held stops a Fencer’s backstep but permits stationary strikes; Muted does not stop physical attacks. The fixed shapes and beat countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 54</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-remains" data-title="Watch what you leave behind"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch what you leave behind</h2><table><tr><td>Afterburst</td><td>The rust-orange zombie fights at close range. Defeating it leaves a fixed orange circle and shrinking bar around its corpse: after 0.8 seconds it bursts once. Back outside the circle or use cover before collecting its ordinary drop. Killing it stops its living attacks, but does not erase this warning.</td></tr><tr><td>Carrion</td><td>The green fast zombie can claim a nearby defeated ally when injured. A crossed-jaw mark, tether and shrinking bar announce 0.6 seconds of stationary feeding. Interrupt, kill or separate it, break the tether with cover, or defeat it before its Shambler escorts. A successful meal restores at most 30 HP, capped at maximum health. It gets one attempt per corpse and waits six seconds before another.</td></tr></table><p>An Afterburst appears with a Soldier; Carrion travels with Shamblers. Carrion can consume an Afterburst before it bursts, extinguishing the circle. Neither action creates another enemy or an extra reward; consumed remains still yield their ordinary drop on schedule.</p><p>Both enemies also warn a narrow, fixed melee sector. Sidestep or interrupt it. Held permits their stationary attacks and feeding; Muted does not stop these physical actions. Circles, tethers and countdowns remain readable with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 55</div></article><article class="page" id="neil" data-title="Neil has the Black Keycard"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Neil has the Black Keycard</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>After Yellow Gate, look for Neil: the green-suited bureaucrat clutching a briefcase. Neil flees through the open labyrinth and across connected floors. He does not attack you. He alone carries the Black Keycard.</p><p>Brute is his independently killable antlion-guard escort. It keeps near Neil and reacts when you hit him. Orange warning marks a charge: after a 1.25-second preparation, Brute commits to its direction. Sidestep that line. A wall collision stuns it for two seconds.</p><p>You may defeat the escort first, but Brute is not an invulnerability switch. Neil can be defeated independently. Take the Black Keycard dropped by Neil to progress.</p><p>Neil is fixed at Dungeon Level. Brute is fixed at Dungeon Level +1.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 56</div></article><article class="page" id="gordon" data-title="Gordon the Warden"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Gordon the Warden</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>Beyond Black Gate is a two-level arena: a lower court and an upper gallery. Gordon is the heavy man in a pig mask. Entering commits the encounter and closes the back gate.</p><p>An entry or respawn alcove provides protection and one aid allowance per Hero identity per dungeon: up to 25 HP, up to 30 ammunition per owned gun, and one Healing Potion. Repeated entry is not an unlimited supply.</p><p>Above roughly 60% HP, Gordon alternates a three-second cloak with a visible warning before launching four slow homing orbs. Around 60%, he rides his toilet and leaves bombs with three-second fuses. Around 25%, he clears that ordnance and charges with his crowbar.</p><p>If his cloaked phase goes twelve seconds without damage, Gordon reveals himself and pauses attacks for a two-second taunt. Hitting him ends the taunt. Crowbar stagger works once every three seconds against Gordon; gun hit stun keeps its usual rules.</p><p>Fake Gordon Clones join at Dungeon Levels 4, 8, 12 and 16, up to four. Each has one-third of the real Gordon’s maximum HP. They spread around the arena and have no boss health bar. Defeating a clone does not release the Jail Key. Defeating the real Gordon ends his fight; at Level 20 it reveals Hector instead of releasing the key.</p><p>The fight does not reset just because a Hero dies. At Levels 1–19 and 21 onward, defeat Gordon, take the Jail Key at the arena center, open the rescue chamber, and reach the damsel—or the cash after Level 20. At Level 20, defeat Hector first. Gordon’s death alone never completes the dungeon.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 57</div></article><article class="page" id="hector" data-title="Hector the Director"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hector the Director</h2><table><tr><td>Level 20 only</td><td>Gordon → Hector → Jail Key → Deborah</td></tr><tr><td>Your target</td><td>The Director’s Heart in the court</td></tr></table><p>When the real Gordon falls at Level 20, an enormous, disturbingly familiar figure rises over the Flattywood horizon. Hector’s three-second reveal begins the final fight. The jail remains locked until you defeat him.</p><p>Attack the Director’s Heart: the red figure at the arena center, tethered to the giant above. This reachable core takes your ordinary weapon and spell hits. The distant body is a spectacle, not a separate target. Fight from the lower court or its upper gallery; the protected entrance permits neither outgoing nor incoming Hector damage.</p><p>Hector opens with blue missile volleys and titanic crowbar strikes. Below roughly 60% health, bombs and purple corrupted Villain of Lore bolts enter the rotation. Below roughly 25%, attacks arrive faster and ground strikes may mark several Heroes. Watch the warning text, leave fixed ground circles before impact, and dodge the traveling orbs.</p><p>Death and revival use the normal Hero rules. Returning Heroes resume the same fight; Hector does not heal or restart because everyone temporarily leaves the court. His attacks stop while no visible, living Hero is available there. The dungeon’s collapse clock still matters.</p><p>Hector grants ordinary combat credit and enemy drops, with no separate currency prize. After his defeat, take the Jail Key, open Deborah’s chamber, and rescue her to finish Level 20. The existing Level 21-and-beyond SECURE THE BAG campaign then continues.</p><p>The familiar Hermit is revealed as Hector the Director. Only after his defeat and your successful rescue of Deborah does the finite rescue story end. Deborah succeeds him in staging; the campaign continues into SECURE THE BAG.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 58</div></article><article class="page" id="finale" data-title="The rescue story is won"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The rescue story is won</h2><p>Deborah’s legitimate rescue begins a 6.5-second celebration inside the normal 20-second victory window. The rescued damsels cheer, and surviving participating Heroes receive her thanks and affectionate celebration beats. Your rescue rewards settle immediately; the celebration grants no extra reward.</p><p>The opening announces Deborah’s rescue, followed by the Hero celebration, then THE RESCUE STORY IS WON. Your actual Hero stays in place. Movement briefly pauses for the participating survivors, then returns. F can open the usual optional Tetris throughout the victory window and dismisses the finale view.</p><p>Reduced effects keeps your ordinary view and the story titles. If you join late, you see the current moment. Missing actors, a death or a reconnect cannot restart the celebration or pay the rescue again.</p><p>At the usual end of the victory window, return to staging for Dungeon 21. Deborah occupies the former Hermit’s position and offers Abundance through the same once-per-account, rolling 24-hour claim. Prepare your equipment, then use the portal to SECURE THE BAG.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 59</div></article><article class="page" id="fund" data-title="The Debbie Fund"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The Debbie Fund</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>The Debbie Fund takes rescue funding very seriously. Its $DEB units are Debbie Coins—&#x27;Debs&#x27;—and its collectibles are Debbie Fund Tokens, or DFTs. These are server-local game records, with no cash value or outside wallet.</p><p>A successful rescue creates a pool of 100 × Dungeon Level Debs. Qualifying Soldier-attributable Hero life losses assign 11% each to the Soldier side, capped at 33% of the pool; Soldier damage contributions determine its allocation.</p><p>The remaining Hero pool is divided half equally and half by damage and effective allied healing, with hostile-loss adjustments. Eligible dead or disconnected participants can retain their claim. Switching to Soldier play forfeits a Hero share.</p><p>Rescue awards increase wallet balance and lifetime rescue earnings. DFT sales increase spendable balance but do not inflate that lifetime rescue score. The staging Heroes of Legend board records the highest dungeon reached, damsels rescued, and cash bags recovered; it is not a count of monster kills.</p><p>At Debbie, choose Fuse Equipment, Sell Equipment, Fuse DFTs, or Sell DFTs. Drag eligible items into the labeled pile, review the value, and confirm. You can also click items to add or remove them. Closing the menu cancels staging without moving anything. Sales award the displayed value in $DEB. Fusion consumes two to eight inputs and returns one legal item worth 85–100% of their combined value and more than any single input; unsupported piles are rejected intact. Equipment fusion returns carried gear; DFT fusion returns a persistent upgraded token. DFT-created gear and free starting weapons cannot be sold or fused. If any fused DFT was recreated this run, the new token remains used for this run. DFT Collection / Recreate retains the free recreation service.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 60</div></article><article class="page" id="tokens" data-title="A little something to keep"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little something to keep</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>DFTs normally mint when a dungeon objective is completed. Combat Level milestones 1, 5, 10, and 20 can each award one token per account on this server. A rare rescue roll also gives each participating Hero a one-in-1,000 chance. Ordinary enemy kills do not mint DFTs. The treasure chest event can also award DFTs; see Treasure to keep. Rescuing Deborah at Level 20 also unlocks Abundance: visit her in staging for one free DFT per Steam account every rolling 24 hours. Reconnecting or restarting a campaign does not reset the timer. The eight-token capacity still applies; a full wallet leaves the gift available until you make room. Recreated equipment retains the usual resale and fusion exclusions.</p><p>Your collection has eight slots. Milestone items waiting for space stay frozen; making room does not reroll them. Tokens persist across ordinary death and campaign restart.</p><p>At the staging Debbie statue, recreate a token&#x27;s frozen item for free once per token per run. The DFT remains in your collection. The same token becomes available to recreate again in the next run.</p><p>You may instead sell a DFT permanently for its displayed Value in Debs. Read the confirmation carefully: selling the token removes it. Wallet viewing is portable; the statue&#x27;s staging, proximity, and role rules still govern its physical services.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 61</div></article><article class="page" id="dungeon-events" data-title="Dungeon Events: discoveries and obstacles"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dungeon Events: discoveries and obstacles</h2><table><tr><td>Slot machine · $5 DEB stake</td><td>One ordinary d4: 4 pays $15 gross (+$10 net); 1–3 pays nothing (−$5 net).</td></tr><tr><td>Odds</td><td>25% win; 75% loss. No exploding dice or ability bonuses.</td></tr></table><p>An enabled dungeon contains 1–4 different event types, chosen by one ordinary, non-exploding d4. A result of 1–3 selects that many from Debbie Slots, locked loot chests, vending machines, False-floor hazards, bribe blockades, Skeleton of a Hero blockades, and the Game Master’s Equipment Quiz; warp holes join this common pool from Dungeon Level 5 onward. A 4 selects three common types plus a DFT treasure event. Treasure therefore appears on 25% of event selections; its one or two physical chests count as one event type. A paired warp hole also counts once. Events never replace the required rescue objective.</p><p>Debbie Slots is optional and never blocks the rescue route. Read its posted stake and odds before pressing E to play. Each Steam account may complete one play per dungeon; reconnecting or beginning a replacement Hero does not grant another play. Other Heroes have their own attempt.</p><p>The stake uses your persistent, server-local $DEB balance, which has no cash value. A completed play applies the stake and payout together. Insufficient funds or a storage failure spends nothing and leaves your attempt available; retrying does not reroll the outcome. Leaving the dungeon ends access to that machine.</p><p>Events are enabled by default on new server configurations. Servers retaining an earlier disabled setting need an administrator to set lod_events_enabled 1 for future dungeon generation.</p><p>In developer mode, administrators can run lod_event_population_preview to generate a normal 1–4 event selection and print its locations, or lod_event_preview_generate followed by an event name for a single-event preview. Previews mark the campaign unranked but still use carried keys and persistent server-local funds and DFTs; they provide no free currency. Regenerating the same dungeon does not refresh account claims.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 62</div></article><article class="page" id="locked-chest" data-title="A key, or a Rogue’s touch"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A key, or a Rogue’s touch</h2><table><tr><td>Chest Key</td><td>Bag-only consumable; stacks up to nine. Press E at a locked chest to use one.</td></tr><tr><td>Rogue lockpick</td><td>Press E without a key, or hold Sprint + E to pick while carrying keys.</td></tr><tr><td>Success chance</td><td>5% per Combat Level, up to 95%; one ordinary d100 roll.</td></tr></table><p>The locked loot chest sits off the required rescue route and offers each Steam account one ordinary procedural wearable per dungeon. It does not award a DFT; the item uses your normal equipment storage.</p><p>Chest Keys can appear among normal loot drops and remain in your bag rather than the Throwable slot. A key is spent only when its chest item successfully enters your equipment storage. A full bag or failed delivery spends no key; make room or retry.</p><p>A Rogue gets one lockpick attempt per chest. Failure uses that attempt but leaves any key intact, and you may later open the chest with a key. A full bag or another rejected preflight does not use the attempt. If a successful pick cannot deliver the item, retry to claim the same reward without another roll or a key.</p><p>The chest remembers your account’s claim and lockpick attempt through reconnects, replacement Heroes, and regeneration of the same dungeon. Those actions cannot grant another attempt or reroll the reward. A new dungeon provides a new opportunity; a new campaign resets the chest record.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 63</div></article><article class="page" id="treasure-chest" data-title="Treasure to keep"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Treasure to keep</h2><table><tr><td>DFT treasure event</td><td>One ordinary d2 selects one or two optional chests; each chest offers each Steam account one DFT.</td></tr><tr><td>Collection capacity</td><td>Eight DFTs. Ordinary equipment bag space does not limit this reward.</td></tr><tr><td>Opening the lock</td><td>Use a Chest Key, or use the same Rogue lockpicking controls and chance as an ordinary locked chest.</td></tr></table><p>Treasure chests hold persistent Debbie Fund Tokens, not ordinary equipment pickups. Each Hero has an independent claim at each chest. The reward is fixed: reconnecting, replacing your Hero, or regenerating the same dungeon cannot reroll it or restore a claim. Selling or recreating the awarded DFT does not reopen the chest.</p><p>Press E to spend one Chest Key when the DFT is successfully delivered. A Rogue without a key may pick the lock; hold Sprint + E to choose lockpicking while carrying keys. Each account gets one attempt per chest at 5% per Combat Level, up to 95%. A failed pick consumes the attempt but no key; you can return with a key.</p><p>A full DFT collection spends neither key nor lockpick attempt. Make room in your collection, then return. A storage failure spends no key. If a successful pick cannot deliver its DFT, the chest stays unlocked for your account: retry for the same reward without another roll or key.</p><p>Treasure events appear only on a four-event selection and contain one or two optional chests. Their DFTs persist in your server-local collection and have no cash value. Leaving the dungeon ends access to its chests. For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate treasure_chest; lod_chest_key_testkit supplies one key only if you have none.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 64</div></article><article class="page" id="vending-machine" data-title="A bottle for the road"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A bottle for the road</h2><table><tr><td>Healing Potion · $10 DEB</td><td>One bottle per Steam account per campaign dungeon. Each Hero has an independent purchase.</td></tr><tr><td>Potion effect</td><td>Restores 25 HP and cures negative statuses when used. Buying the bottle does not heal you.</td></tr><tr><td>Carrying capacity</td><td>Healing Potions stack up to three and use the Throwable slot.</td></tr></table><p>Press E at a vending machine to buy one standard Healing Potion for your equipment inventory. The price uses your persistent, server-local $DEB balance, which has no cash value. The potion is ordinary run equipment, not a DFT.</p><p>Insufficient funds, a full potion stack, a storage failure, or leaving the interaction before it can complete spends nothing and leaves your purchase available. Correct the problem and retry. A successful purchase charges exactly $10 DEB and delivers one bottle.</p><p>The machine remembers your account’s completed purchase through death, replacement Heroes, reconnects, and regeneration of the same dungeon. Drinking or discarding the bottle does not restock the machine, and reconnecting does not replace a lost bottle. A new dungeon or campaign provides a new purchase.</p><p>This optional machine can appear alongside other Dungeon Events. For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate vending_machine. Purchases spend real server-local $DEB; the preview does not provide free currency.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 65</div></article><article class="page" id="false-floor" data-title="Watch your step"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch your step</h2><p>A False-floor hazard is a small panel in an optional upper-floor room. Stepping fully onto it as a grounded Hero can open the floor beneath you. There is no E prompt to activate it. The solid rim around the panel gives you room to go around.</p><p>The fall leads one floor down. Ordinary gravity and falling consequences apply. The destination preserves your current progression access, and the existing stairs provide a route back; the trap never opens a locked progression shortcut.</p><p>The panel stays open for at least three seconds. It closes and rearms only after its opening is clear, so keep moving after landing. All Heroes share the same panel state, and the hazard can trigger again. If the landing is unsafe or occupied before activation, the panel stays closed.</p><p>For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate false_floor. Redeploy after generation and follow the printed locator.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 66</div></article><article class="page" id="warp-hole" data-title="A shorter way back"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A shorter way back</h2><p>From Dungeon Level 5 onward, a paired warp hole may appear as an optional event. Its two cyan endpoints sit on different physical floors. The prompt identifies your endpoint and its destination floor.</p><p>Press E at either dormant endpoint. Your first successful traversal links the pair for everyone until this dungeon ends. Linked holes are free and repeatable; wait one second between your own traversals. Other Heroes can use them independently.</p><p>A warp hole shortens a route you can already reach. It never skips a locked gate, required boss, key or rescue step, and the ordinary stairs still provide a way back. It preserves your facing and stops your previous momentum.</p><p>If the arrival is occupied or unsafe, you stay where you are. Clear the landing and try again. A refused first traversal leaves the pair dormant. Regeneration replaces both endpoints with a fresh dormant pair.</p><p>For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate warp_hole. This unranked single-event preview can show the pair before Dungeon Level 5. Redeploy after generation and follow the printed locators.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 67</div></article><article class="page" id="bribe-blockade" data-title="Pay the toll — or find the collateral"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Pay the toll — or find the collateral</h2><p>A bribe blockade closes a required route until one Hero surrenders at least 50 $DEB of equipment value. It opens for the whole party. No cash leaves your wallet; excess item value is not returned as change.</p><p>You always have a route to a lost-property cache on the approachable side. Press E at the cache to earmark its generated ring as shared collateral. The ring stays safely in the cache until payment: it cannot be worn, sold, discarded or lost. Finding it never consumes your equipment.</p><p>Press E at the toll terminal to review payment. Choose the recovered ring, or select 1–8 of your own unequipped procedural wearables worth at least 50 in total. Weapons, consumables, protected or free starting gear a]====]
